title: 'Mod playbooks idea, checkpoint 1: where it stands now'
summary: |-
  The founder sharpened the idea: "each mod has a set of protocols that an agent can easily understand the use cases of when and where it works or when to suggest it, like a playbook… akin to how mcp is to cli". They also want mods to have "their own ui in the gnomon display for a more localised experience (at times)", and "the protocol around it for users to make and share their own mods around other repos and flows".
  Current shape: mod = playbook (Agent Skills-style: frontmatter description = when to use/suggest; full body on demand) + capabilities (MCP or kernel calls) + optional local UI (panel or fenced sheet) + handoff contracts (context in, results out, chainable). The agent offers mods by conversation phase (diverge → synthesize → commit) as invitation chips/voice, accepted by a trusted gesture.
  Key open risk: a third-party playbook is text inside the agent's context, so it is a prompt-injection channel that needs trust tiers and sanitisation.
  In flight: Arky (protocol, runtime, security, sharing, phased plan) and Luna (invitation, handoff and local-UI boards).
  The founder asked for lore at each step and at the end.
